            <div xml:id="d531">

                <pb facs="0585" n="577" sameAs="frus:frus1939v02/pg577"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.4016/2051: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Germany</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gilbert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-01-06T13:00:00+01:00">January 6, 1939—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 6—9:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>13. Embassy’s telegrams 764, December 29, 1 p.m.<anchor n="41" xml:id="d531-f1"/>
                    and 6, January 3, 5 p.m.</p>
                <p>The following third-person note, dated December 31, has been received today from
                    the Foreign Office.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“In reply to <hi rendition="#italic">note verbale</hi> No. 274 of December
                        29, 1938,<anchor n="40" xml:id="d531-f2"/> the Foreign Office has the honor
                        to inform the Embassy of the United States of America that the assurances
                        given the Government of the United States in <hi rendition="#italic">note
                            verbale</hi> Number 84–60 SHT. 14–12 of December 30, with regard to the
                        examination and consideration of treaty rights to which American citizens
                        are entitled, apply also to the present inquiry.”</p>
                </quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gilbert</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
